      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1626.0419478503716,
      "y": 963.876135907863,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 809.079345703125,
      "height": 120,
      "seed": 927419533,
      "groupIds": [],
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786531599122,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "text": "If each Service is running as a single threaded program, and we had CPU affinity\n(which we don't), we could simplify our visualization of 4 services running on a 2\nCPU node sharing time like this. With this configuration we are leveraging the 2\nCPUs with maximum efficiency. Context switches only take place when it's deemed\nnecessary.",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "If each Service is running as a single threaded program, and we had CPU affinity\n(which we don't), we could simplify our visualization of 4 services running on a 2\nCPU node sharing time like this. With this configuration we are leveraging the 2\nCPUs with maximum efficiency. Context switches only take place when it's deemed\nnecessary.",
      "index": "b0X",
      "frameId": null,
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.2
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 5823,
      "versionNonce": 485130468,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "QMJWZeg52PJJ1g_wKrSi6",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1649.6197857944187,
      "y": 1758.2399820178375,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 937.6281127929688,
      "height": 319.2,
      "seed": 311640621,
      "groupIds": [],
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786531599122,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 19.905658298068573,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "text": "In reality it's much more complicated than this because when services are running with multiple\nthreads, all the threads will be scheduled to run on the available CPUs and the sum of those\nrunning threads per process will be regulated to the limit value.\n\nA limit at or below `1000m` (100ms) means the service will only use a single CPU of time on\nevery cycle. For services written in Go, this means the service needs to be configured as a\nsingle threaded Go program. If not, then the service will not run as efficiently as it otherwise\ncould. This is because Go programs run as CPU bound programs. This inefficiency gets worse\nthe more CPUs that exist on the node since the Go runtime creates a thread per CPU by\ndefault.\n\nThis visualization tries to capture a single cycle of services, each running with four threads,\non a four CPU node, with the same limits as the last example. You can see limits are hit four\ntimes quicker, with extra context switching, resulting in less work getting done over time.",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "In reality it's much more complicated than this because when services are running with multiple\nthreads, all the threads will be scheduled to run on the available CPUs and the sum of those\nrunning threads per process will be regulated to the limit value.\n\nA limit at or below `1000m` (100ms) means the service will only use a single CPU of time on\nevery cycle. For services written in Go, this means the service needs to be configured as a\nsingle threaded Go program. If not, then the service will not run as efficiently as it otherwise\ncould. This is because Go programs run as CPU bound programs. This inefficiency gets worse\nthe more CPUs that exist on the node since the Go runtime creates a thread per CPU by\ndefault.\n\nThis visualization tries to capture a single cycle of services, each running with four threads,\non a four CPU node, with the same limits as the last example. You can see limits are hit four\ntimes quicker, with extra context switching, resulting in less work getting done over time.",
      "index": "b0Y",
      "frameId": null,
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.1454029632475036
    },
